	"identifying_description": "Two commercially available Everlast boxing gloves with adjustable Velcro cuffs. The gloves are made of black leather for the main body, white leather trim and black machine stiches. There is a polyester satin label on each wrist. Each glove is supported at the interior with a block (redwood?) covered with leather. Exterior of gloves are decorated with cowrie shells and red, white and blue seed beads, and a small round mirror on each palm surrounded by blue faceted beads, mother of pearl beads and red seed beads. The back of the right glove reads \"Save Me\" and the back of the left glove reads \"Joe Louis\". Each adjustable cuff has a black elastic band that is attached to the block with a tack. Gloves are connected to each other with a stainless steal chain that has U loops on each end screwed into blocks. The chain is decorated with a red synthetic ribbon, red and clear seed beads, clear bugle beads and cowrie shells. Near the center: a D shaped carabiner is looped through one of the chain links. The artist has indicated that this is not part of the artwork and possibly came from the gallery and was used for handling (Rodriguez/Broxton communication 7/18/23). There is small (1-inch) oval carabiner attached to the chain that indicates the intended center for installation. When installed, the glove reading 'Save Me' hangs higher than the glove reading 'Joe Louis' as per artist instructions. (Rodriguez/Broxton communication 7/18/23).",
	"web_text": "Demetri Broxton sees his work as an ongoing investigation into cultural continuities from Africa to America. Emphasizing traditions of the people of Yoruba, Nigeria, and the ancient kingdom of Kongo, Save Me, Joe Louis features boxing gloves that hang from steel chains adorned with shells, beads, and threads evocative of dripping blood. The embroidered words on the gloves refer to a story made known by Martin Luther King Jr. of a Black man on death row who invoked the power of the legendary African American boxer as he drew his last breath. Evoking the textile traditions of New Orleans’s Black Maskers, this work represents the artist’s liberatory approach to history and materials.",
	"didactic_label": "Demetri Broxton sees his work as an ongoing investigation into cultural continuities from Africa to America. Emphasizing traditions of the people of Yoruba, Nigeria, and the ancient kingdom of Kongo, Save Me, Joe Louis features boxing gloves that hang from steel chains adorned with shells, beads, and threads evocative of dripping blood. The embroidered words on the gloves refer to a story made known by Martin Luther King Jr. of a Black man on death row who invoked the power of the legendary African American boxer as he drew his last breath. Evoking the textile traditions of New Orleans’s Black Maskers, this work represents the artist’s liberatory approach to history and materials.",
